WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.020
How do you find one gene when its DNA fragment hides in a smear?

2
00:00:05.420 --> 00:00:11.020
Cut genomic DNA with a restriction enzyme, a sequence-specific cutter, and a gel

3
00:00:11.020 --> 00:00:12.280
shows a crowded blur.

4
00:00:13.580 --> 00:00:16.540
To spot one, you need both its size and its identity.

5
00:00:19.650 --> 00:00:22.730
First, load the cut DNA into an agarose gel.

6
00:00:23.990 --> 00:00:28.030
Its negatively charged backbone moves toward the positive electrode.

7
00:00:29.590 --> 00:00:31.890
Smaller fragments travel faster through the mesh.

8
00:00:33.130 --> 00:00:36.930
A size ladder in a neighboring lane turns position into length.

9
00:00:37.810 --> 00:00:41.210
Still, a right-sized band shows size, not identity.

10
00:00:42.333 --> 00:00:48.353
In 1975, Edwin Southern added identity without losing the gel's separation.

11
00:00:49.793 --> 00:00:54.113
DNA in the gel is denatured into single strands so a probe can pair.

12
00:00:55.533 --> 00:01:00.473
Capillary flow transfers that exact pattern onto a cellulose nitrate membrane.

13
00:01:01.773 --> 00:01:07.073
A labeled probe washes across, sticking only to matching, complementary sequences.

14
00:01:08.033 --> 00:01:13.413
The gel sorts by size; pairing with the probe, hybridization, reveals identity.

15
00:01:14.583 --> 00:01:21.083
In 1977, James Alwine, David Kemp and George Stark adapted transfer for RNA.

16
00:01:22.523 --> 00:01:26.383
Its name, Northern blot, began as a joke on Southern's name.

17
00:01:27.723 --> 00:01:33.183
RNA separates by size, transfers to a membrane, and binds a labeled DNA probe.

18
00:01:34.743 --> 00:01:39.723
Band intensity reports how much messenger RNA of that gene the cells contain.

19
00:01:40.867 --> 00:01:43.187
Test this on a chromosome's two copies.

20
00:01:44.487 --> 00:01:50.167
Suppose an enzyme cuts one copy's version, or allele, into a long piece, and the

21
00:01:50.167 --> 00:01:51.507
other's into a short piece.

22
00:01:52.807 --> 00:01:55.667
Normal tissue shows both bands on a Southern blot.

23
00:01:56.927 --> 00:02:00.207
If a tumor cell loses one allele, what will its lane show?

24
00:02:05.750 --> 00:02:09.030
Only one band appears: the tumor lost one allele.

25
00:02:10.490 --> 00:02:14.330
Still, brightness alone can mislead, since uneven loading shifts it.

26
00:02:15.490 --> 00:02:20.290
A size ladder calibrates length, and a positive control shows the assay works.

27
00:02:21.630 --> 00:02:26.670
A loading control, like a normal-copy gene or stable-RNA reprobe, supports

28
00:02:26.670 --> 00:02:29.670
comparison only while brightness still tracks amount.

29
00:02:30.800 --> 00:02:37.380
In 1983, Webster Cavenee, Thaddeus Dryja and colleagues compared normal and tumor

30
00:02:37.380 --> 00:02:39.260
DNA in retinoblastoma.

31
00:02:40.540 --> 00:02:45.380
In several patients, the tumor showed only one of two chromosome 13 alleles.

32
00:02:46.333 --> 00:02:52.873
In 1987, Dennis Slamon and colleagues probed 189 primary breast cancers.

33
00:02:54.413 --> 00:02:59.453
Band strength against a normal-copy control gene reported HER2 copies.

34
00:03:00.793 --> 00:03:06.253
About 30 percent carried extra copies; those women relapsed and died sooner, most

35
00:03:06.253 --> 00:03:07.853
clearly with lymph-node spread.

36
00:03:08.917 --> 00:03:14.877
In 1996, William Kaelin and colleagues ran Northern blots on kidney-cancer cells.

37
00:03:16.117 --> 00:03:19.237
Some lacked the VHL gene; others had it restored.

38
00:03:20.577 --> 00:03:25.257
Without it, VEGF and PDGF-B messenger RNA stayed high.

39
00:03:26.497 --> 00:03:27.837
So did GLUT1.

40
00:03:28.850 --> 00:03:31.830
That held at 21 and 1 percent oxygen.

41
00:03:32.770 --> 00:03:35.970
So VHL is needed for the oxygen dependence.

42
00:03:37.510 --> 00:03:40.150
Size sorts the smear; a probe names the sequence.
